---
import { demoKeyless } from "../../../data/demo-evidence.ts";
import { demoGate, gateComparison, rules } from "../../../data/docs.ts";
import { finalGate } from "../../../data/final-evidence.ts";

const plural = (n: number): string => `${n} ${n === 1 ? "day" : "days"}`;

/**
 * Limits, in plain words. Grounded in docs/PRD.md "Risks and guarantees", README.md ("Live demo gate",
 * "Verifier deployment and trust limits"), crates/verifier/src/LOCAL.md ("One worker runs at a time")
 * and docs/technical/ARCHITECTURE.md (admission and cool-down table). Per-rule limits come from the
 * same data as the rules section, so the two cannot drift.
 */
---

<div class="docs-prose">
  <h3>What this is</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Testnet only.</strong> Everything runs on NEAR testnet and Base Sepolia. Nothing
      here holds real value.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not audited.</strong> The project's security reviews are internal. None of them is an
      independent audit, and no production security claim follows from its tests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The result of a run is an observation.</strong> A refusal leaves the proxy unchanged.
      An outage of the verifier, the AI or the MPC network only delays an upgrade; it never
      produces a signature without a certificate.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The verifier</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One job at a time.</strong> Each gate has a single verifier machine with one worker. A
      request that arrives while it is busy is refused with a retry delay, so you may have to
      try again after someone else's run finishes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anyone can take the worker.</strong> Admission is permissionless: anyone holding a valid
      sealed bundle for a Pending proposal can start its run.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cool-down after a failed run.</strong> A failed or withheld run delays the next one for
      that proposal: 10, 20, then 40 minutes, capped at one hour. Faults of the verifier itself do not count.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Speed is historical.</strong> The four demo attacks took 35 to 117 seconds on a warm
      machine, and the final gate's honest upgrade with AI review took 515.8 seconds. Neither is a
      promise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Availability is not promised.</strong> An endpoint may go offline. A restart changes the
      certificate key, and registering a replacement needs a timelocked change. Check the machine
      with <code>verifier appraise</code> before you seal private source to it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Know the boundary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One proxy.</strong> Only the configured proxy is covered, and only once its upgrade
      authority was fully migrated to the gate. A proxy whose authority was never migrated is not
      protected.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everything beside the gate.</strong> Keys and allowances outside it, the frontend
      supply chain, unrelated contracts, Safe arbitrary signing and malware on a signing device
      stay outside what these rules can stop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trust that remains.</strong> MPC honesty and availability, the TEE vendor and its
      attestation, the RPC evidence policy, and the availability of the verifier and of inference.
      A required review that does not finish withholds the certificate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Governance is delayed, not impossible.</strong> A governor can schedule public
      changes and a guardian can pause or cancel them, and a hostile guardian can halt signing
      indefinitely. The delay is {plural(demoGate.governanceDelayDays)} on the demo gate and {" "}
      {plural(finalGate.governanceDelayDays)} on the final gate, and no CLI switch removes these
      assumptions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyless is claimed per gate.</strong> The demo gate's key was deleted on {" "}
      {demoKeyless.date} and its audit exited {demoKeyless.auditExitCode}. That says nothing about any
      other deployment, so read the current audit of the one you rely on.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What the rules do not prove</h3>
  <p>
    Rules protect only what they declare. A flaw outside R1 to R5, or beyond their bounds, can pass.
    The limits of each rule are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      rules.map((rule) => (
        <li>
          <strong>
            {rule.id} {rule.name}.
          </strong>{" "}
          {rule.limit}
        </li>
      ))
    }
    <li>
      <strong>Block environment.</strong> R2 runs under one fixed block environment, and R3 does not
      forbid block-environment opcodes. A candidate whose drain depends on a later time or block
      value can pass.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your initializer.</strong> R2 starts from a fixed initializer and fixed actors, not
      from your exact initializer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The R5 context.</strong> R5 depends on authenticated witnessed state and a pinned
      header policy. It replays with an all-zero certificate id and the maximum deadline, which an
      initializer could tell apart from a real certificate, and it does not compare gas.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The AI review.</strong> It is nondeterministic. It can refuse with a counterexample the
      verifier reproduces, and it can never approve. A timeout or error withholds certification, so an
      honest deterministic pass alone cannot sign while the required review is incomplete.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attestation.</strong> It shows which code ran on which input. It does not show that the
      conclusion is right.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>One vault, one rule set</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The only active rule set is the Vault pack, and it guards one vault proxy. A candidate with
      constructor arguments, immutables or unresolved library links may fail runtime matching.
    </li>
    <li>Rule-pack presets and a rule-pack builder are planned. They do not exist yet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Demo gate and final gate</h3>
  <p>
    They run identical gate code with different rule sets. The final gate holds the permanent
    recorded evidence. The demo gate exists so you can run attacks yourself, and what you run there
    never appears in the final gate's evidence.
  </p>
  <dl class="docs-compare">
    {
      gateComparison.map((row) => (
        <div class="docs-compare__row">
          <dt>{row.term}</dt>
          <dd>
            <span>Final gate</span>
            <span>{row.final}</span>
          </dd>
          <dd>
            <span>Demo gate</span>
            <span>{row.demo}</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
</div>
